<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  This accent is genuinely under the app's control, not OS/OEM chrome — a neutral gray was
  tried here on a mistaken belief that it was, and on-device testing showed the colorized card
  rendering blue regardless of that gray, which read as proof it couldn't be changed. It wasn't
  OEM chrome: a colorized notification's background comes from setColor() in Notifications.kt,
  but Android's colorized-card algorithm needs an actual hue to hold onto for contrast, and a
  near-zero-saturation gray like the one previously here gives it nothing to work with, so it
  fell back to the device's own Material You accent (blue, on the device this was tested on)
  instead. The fix is a real, saturated color, not "no color".

  This is Palette.CONCEPT's tertiary — the coral pinned to "an armed alarm" in Palettes.kt's own
  doc comment — so the notification card, the status-bar chip, and the in-app alarm accent all
  read as the same brand color instead of an unrelated device-picked one.
-->
<resources>
    <color name="notification_accent">#B3263E</color>
</resources>
